					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/kalinin" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2203795); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=2203795&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1610118758" alt="Kalinin" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=2203795)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/kalinin" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2203795); return false;" >Kalinin</a></span> <span class="odate time_1479757058 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">21 Nov 2016 19:37</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2695668">
									

<p>Not bad. The initial concept and the bit about the Americans with Disabilities Act were pretty neat, although I would have preferred if it were an open question as to whether there really was a guy with 300&#160;m-long fingers running around somewhere. Would have served as a nice piece of fridge logic. I'll second Scan's summation above of &quot;deliciously weird&quot; though. Nicely uncanny.</p>
<p>Two issues. As others have said, the picture probably needs to go, both from compliance issues and the fact that it's obviously computer generated, which breaks immersion for me. I would imagine that it would be possible to achieve a similar effect in photoshop. Also, the Euclidean geometry thing. The regular, non-anomalous world exists in a non-Euclidean setting. Somewhere along the line, &quot;non-Euclidean&quot; became a byword for &quot;crazy dimensional hammerspace,&quot; but that's not accurate.</p>

							</div>

								
					
