			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2695718">
									

<p>Just for the record, the term for &quot;bigger on the inside&quot; geometry is <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hyperbolic_Space">Hyperbolic Space</a>.</p>
<p>The confusion over the name actually goes back to the early 1900's: In Lovecraft's day, non-euclidean geometry <em>did</em> just mean &quot;crazy dimensional hammerspace&quot; (that doesn't follow one or more of the rules of normal euclidean geometry), but they've since formalized and subdivided the field, and now it just refers to curved spaces- elliptical if parallel lines can converge, and hyperbolic if they can diverge.</p>

